1.23.4: the wipe is two images and a divider, nothing else

The wipe's base image was never clipped: left of the divider it showed
the left image where that had pixels and the view everywhere else, so
with a Polaroid on either side the original bled through around the
print. That was the fundamental fault behind every report.

The composer now builds one frame from the two images: the union of
their extents in the view's output pixels, aligned on the picture
(left pixel u is shown pixel u - placeL + cropL - cropS + placeS),
fitted to the viewport or at 1:1. The view is clipped to the right of
the divider and the left image to the left of it; where either has no
pixels the stage shows through. The divider runs over that frame, the
loupe looks at the left image left of it, and captions follow the
view (a render landing while the original is on view no longer
relabels it).

And the view itself: after choosing an image the page showed the
original and the first render never brought the view to "current" -
the guard meant for the \-key glance blocked it - while the caption
said "rendered". So a good part of what looked reversed was the
original on view with a render's caption. A render now brings the
view to current unless the glance key is held, and the caption
follows the view.

Tests: the Chromium placement check runs on the composed frame (54
cases, both pictures' centers coinciding within 0.75 px); the divider
at 25% must clip the view's left quarter; a render under the original
view keeps caption and view.

